From: Andrei Lepikhov <andrei.lepikhov@pgedge.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Demo planner probe: raise a flag on a self outer join

Debugging-only core patch for contrib/pg_probe_explain, an alternative to
0001 and 0002 - all three export the same boolean, so apply one of them, not
several.

It teaches the self-join elimination code to report the case it deliberately
refuses to handle: two scans of the same table sitting on opposite sides of an
outer join, tied together by a self-join condition ("X = X").
remove_self_joins_one_group() throws such a pair away as soon as it sees that
the two relations are not on the same side of every special join.  Here the
pair survives that check for a moment longer: if the two relations are exactly
the two sides of that special join, probe_self_outer_join() looks at the
condition of the join, raises planner_probe_hit when it finds a self-join
qual, and only then the pair is dropped, exactly as before.  Nothing about
planning changes; `make check` passes unmodified.

Where the condition is found depends on the kind of join.  A mergejoinable
"outervar = innervar" qual of a LEFT, RIGHT or FULL join is set aside by
deconstruct_jointree() in root->left_join_clauses and friends, each entry
carrying the SpecialJoinInfo it belongs to - the most precise source there is.
Everything else, the quals of an anti join in particular, is in the joininfo
lists of the two relations by then.  Both are fed to split_selfjoin_quals(),
the routine the elimination code itself uses to tell "x = x" from "a = b".

FULL joins need one more thing: deconstruct_jointree() makes each of their
sides a subproblem of its own, so the two never turn up in one group in
remove_self_joins_recurse() and the probe there never sees them.
probe_full_self_joins() walks root->join_info_list instead and hands every
same-table pair of a FULL join to the same routine.

What is *not* reported: semi joins (their quals are ordinary join quals and
get absorbed by an equivalence class, leaving the probe nothing to look at -
and a unique-inner semi join is reduced to an inner join before this code runs
anyway), and anything at all when enable_self_join_elimination is off, since
that switch turns off the pass this probe lives in.

No uniqueness proof is attempted.  The question asked is "does this query
contain a self outer join performed on a self-join condition", not "could that
join be eliminated" - which is the whole point, since the answer to the second
one is currently always "no".

Applies to REL_18_STABLE (tested at 051db7737c1, 18.6).
---
diff --git a/src/backend/optimizer/plan/analyzejoins.c b/src/backend/optimizer/plan/analyzejoins.c
index df7796b..604a663 100644
--- a/src/backend/optimizer/plan/analyzejoins.c
+++ b/src/backend/optimizer/plan/analyzejoins.c
@@ -99,6 +99,9 @@ static void fixup_selfjoin_jointree(PlannerInfo *root, Node *jtnode, int relid,
 static List *fixup_selfjoin_quals(PlannerInfo *root, List *quals, int relid);
 static Node *replace_selfjoin_qual(Node *qual);
 static int	self_join_candidates_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);
+static void probe_self_outer_join(PlannerInfo *root, RelOptInfo *rrel,
+								  RelOptInfo *krel, SpecialJoinInfo *sjinfo);
+static void probe_full_self_joins(PlannerInfo *root);
 
 
 /*
@@ -1847,6 +1850,7 @@ remove_self_joins_one_group(PlannerInfo *root, Relids relids)
 			List	   *otherjoinquals;
 			ListCell   *lc;
 			bool		jinfo_check = true;
+			SpecialJoinInfo *probe_sjinfo = NULL;
 			PlanRowMark *kmark = NULL;
 			PlanRowMark *rmark = NULL;
 			List	   *uclauses = NIL;
@@ -1870,11 +1874,34 @@ remove_self_joins_one_group(PlannerInfo *root, Relids relids)
 					 bms_is_member(r, info->syn_righthand)))
 				{
 					jinfo_check = false;
+
+					/*
+					 * Probe: remember the special join if these two relations
+					 * are precisely its two sides.  Any other reason for the
+					 * check to fail - one of them sitting inside the other
+					 * side of some unrelated join, say - is of no interest
+					 * here.
+					 */
+					if ((bms_is_member(k, info->syn_lefthand) &&
+						 bms_is_member(r, info->syn_righthand)) ||
+						(bms_is_member(r, info->syn_lefthand) &&
+						 bms_is_member(k, info->syn_righthand)))
+						probe_sjinfo = info;
 					break;
 				}
 			}
 			if (!jinfo_check)
+			{
+				/*
+				 * The join stays, and that is still this routine's answer.
+				 * Before dropping the pair, though, let the probe have a look
+				 * at it: a self join under an outer join is exactly what it
+				 * is asked to report.
+				 */
+				if (probe_sjinfo != NULL)
+					probe_self_outer_join(root, rrel, krel, probe_sjinfo);
 				continue;
+			}
 
 			/*
 			 * Check Row Marks equivalence. We can't remove the join if the
@@ -1988,6 +2015,135 @@ remove_self_joins_one_group(PlannerInfo *root, Relids relids)
 	return removed;
 }
 
+/*
+ * probe_self_outer_join
+ *		Debugging probe: report a self join hidden under an outer join.
+ *
+ * 'rrel' and 'krel' scan the same table and are the two sides of the special
+ * join 'sjinfo'.  If the condition of that join is a self-join condition -
+ * a mergejoinable equality whose two sides are the same expression over the
+ * two relations, the "X = X" that remove_self_joins_one_group() looks for -
+ * raise planner_probe_hit, so that a loadable module can notice it once
+ * planning is over.  Nothing else changes: the caller still refuses to
+ * remove the join.
+ *
+ * Note that no uniqueness proof is attempted here.  The question the probe
+ * asks is "does this query contain a self outer join performed on a
+ * self-join condition", not "could that join be eliminated".
+ */
+static void
+probe_self_outer_join(PlannerInfo *root, RelOptInfo *rrel, RelOptInfo *krel,
+					  SpecialJoinInfo *sjinfo)
+{
+	Relids		joinrelids;
+	List	   *ojclauses[3];
+	List	   *joinquals = NIL;
+	List	   *selfjoinquals;
+	List	   *otherjoinquals;
+	int			i;
+
+	/* The flag is sticky - one report per query is enough */
+	if (planner_probe_hit)
+		return;
+
+	joinrelids = bms_union(rrel->relids, krel->relids);
+
+	/*
+	 * A mergejoinable "outervar = innervar" qual of a LEFT, RIGHT or FULL
+	 * join is set aside by deconstruct_jointree() in one of these lists,
+	 * together with the special join it came from, and is not in the joininfo
+	 * lists at this point.  That makes them the most precise source we have:
+	 * the clause is known to be the join condition of that very join.
+	 */
+	ojclauses[0] = root->left_join_clauses;
+	ojclauses[1] = root->right_join_clauses;
+	ojclauses[2] = root->full_join_clauses;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+	{
+		foreach_node(OuterJoinClauseInfo, ojcinfo, ojclauses[i])
+		{
+			if (ojcinfo->sjinfo != sjinfo)
+				continue;
+			if (!bms_equal(ojcinfo->rinfo->clause_relids, joinrelids))
+				continue;
+
+			joinquals = lappend(joinquals, ojcinfo->rinfo);
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Everything else - the quals of a semi or anti join, and outer join
+	 * quals of shapes the lists above don't cover - has been handed to the
+	 * joininfo lists of the relations they mention.  An outer join qual can't
+	 * be pushed down, so a clause mentioning exactly our two relations is
+	 * applied at this join.
+	 */
+	foreach_node(RestrictInfo, rinfo, rrel->joininfo)
+	{
+		if (!bms_equal(rinfo->clause_relids, joinrelids))
+			continue;
+		if (list_member_ptr(joinquals, rinfo))
+			continue;
+
+		joinquals = lappend(joinquals, rinfo);
+	}
+
+	if (joinquals == NIL)
+		return;
+
+	/* Is any of them an "X = X" over the two relations? */
+	split_selfjoin_quals(root, joinquals, &selfjoinquals, &otherjoinquals,
+						 rrel->relid, krel->relid);
+
+	if (selfjoinquals != NIL)
+		planner_probe_hit = true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * probe_full_self_joins
+ *		Debugging probe: the FULL join case of probe_self_outer_join().
+ *
+ * deconstruct_jointree() makes each side of a FULL join a subproblem of its
+ * own, so the two sides never turn up in one group in
+ * remove_self_joins_recurse() and the probe there never sees them.  Walk the
+ * special joins instead and hand every same-table pair to the probe.
+ */
+static void
+probe_full_self_joins(PlannerInfo *root)
+{
+	foreach_node(SpecialJoinInfo, sjinfo, root->join_info_list)
+	{
+		int			r = -1;
+
+		if (sjinfo->jointype != JOIN_FULL)
+			continue;
+
+		while ((r = bms_next_member(sjinfo->syn_lefthand, r)) > 0)
+		{
+			RelOptInfo *rrel = root->simple_rel_array[r];
+			RangeTblEntry *rrte = root->simple_rte_array[r];
+			int			k = -1;
+
+			if (rrel == NULL || rrte->rtekind != RTE_RELATION)
+				continue;
+
+			while ((k = bms_next_member(sjinfo->syn_righthand, k)) > 0)
+			{
+				RelOptInfo *krel = root->simple_rel_array[k];
+				RangeTblEntry *krte = root->simple_rte_array[k];
+
+				if (krel == NULL || krte->rtekind != RTE_RELATION)
+					continue;
+				if (krte->relid != rrte->relid)
+					continue;
+
+				probe_self_outer_join(root, rrel, krel, sjinfo);
+			}
+		}
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * Gather indexes of base relations from the joinlist and try to eliminate
  * self-joins.
@@ -2171,6 +2327,9 @@ remove_useless_self_joins(PlannerInfo *root, List *joinlist)
 		(list_length(joinlist) == 1 && !IsA(linitial(joinlist), List)))
 		return false;
 
+	/* Probe: the pass below can't see the two sides of a FULL join */
+	probe_full_self_joins(root);
+
 	/* Try to merge pairs of self-joined relations. */
 	return remove_self_joins_recurse(root, joinlist);
 }
diff --git a/src/backend/optimizer/util/pathnode.c b/src/backend/optimizer/util/pathnode.c
index d76f693..4616975 100644
--- a/src/backend/optimizer/util/pathnode.c
+++ b/src/backend/optimizer/util/pathnode.c
@@ -42,6 +42,13 @@ typedef enum
 	COSTS_DIFFERENT,			/* neither path dominates the other on cost */
 } PathCostComparison;
 
+/*
+ * Probe flag reported to loadable modules.  Here it is raised whenever the
+ * planner detects a self join under an outer join; see the comment in
+ * pathnode.h and probe_self_outer_join() in analyzejoins.c.
+ */
+bool		planner_probe_hit = false;
+
 /*
  * STD_FUZZ_FACTOR is the normal fuzz factor for compare_path_costs_fuzzily.
  * XXX is it worth making this user-controllable?  It provides a tradeoff
diff --git a/src/include/optimizer/pathnode.h b/src/include/optimizer/pathnode.h
index 60dcdb7..4814834 100644
--- a/src/include/optimizer/pathnode.h
+++ b/src/include/optimizer/pathnode.h
@@ -17,6 +17,15 @@
 #include "nodes/bitmapset.h"
 #include "nodes/pathnodes.h"
 
+/*
+ * Debugging aid: a probe flag that the planner sets when a condition of
+ * interest is met while planning, so that a loadable module can notice it
+ * once planning is over.  The core only ever sets the flag; clearing it
+ * before planning is the reader's responsibility.  See
+ * contrib/pg_probe_explain.
+ */
+extern PGDLLIMPORT bool planner_probe_hit;
+
 
